The file patches 1 constant of the real Go source.

startDetectingCyclesAfter holds 1000 in Go, and 100 in this build. A cycle of
a pointer, of a map, or of a slice runs through the arshalers of reflect, and
1 level of that descent costs about 7 frames of the machine. The stack of Dart
holds about 2,000 frames, thus the guard of Go would never run.

Cycle tests derive their expected error depth from the constant. The noncyclic
slice test retains its 1000-level input and expected output through
arshal_test.go.patch. A cycle error names a shorter JSON pointer than Go.
Read docs/parity.md.

